# A supersession fork and a plain chain, side by side (TX-P6 step 5).
#
# `fixtures/corpus` has no supersession at all, so the pass of SMYSL-2.5 §3.2 had nothing to
# act on there. This file is the smallest store that exercises both of its arms at once:
#
#   c/first    is superseded once, by c/second, which is superseded by c/third
#              -> a chain, and the pass resolves c/first all the way to c/third
#   c/disputed is superseded by two units that nothing orders
#              -> a fork: no single head, both successors kept, `SMY-W464`
#   c/settled  is superseded by nobody
#              -> untouched, so the pass can be seen to leave alone what it should
#
# It lives here and not in `fixtures/corpus` for the reason `catalog.smy` does: that tree is
# F1 to F13 with an `.expected` beside each, iterated by four other crates' tests, and a
# fourteenth file would change what those tests measure.

@claim c/first { status: speculative }
~ The first reading of the pool regression.

@claim c/second { status: speculative }
~ The second reading, which corrects the first.

@claim c/third { status: speculative }
~ The third reading, which corrects the second.

@claim c/disputed { status: speculative }
~ A claim two people revised at once.

@claim c/revision-a { status: speculative }
~ One revision of the disputed claim.

@claim c/revision-b { status: speculative }
~ Another revision of the disputed claim, written without seeing the first.

@claim c/settled { status: speculative }
~ A claim nobody revised.

@rel c/second --supersedes--> c/first
@rel c/third --supersedes--> c/second
@rel c/revision-a --supersedes--> c/disputed
@rel c/revision-b --supersedes--> c/disputed
